1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,240
Imagine burning, uh, $10 million

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:09,220
and six months of developer time only to realize the vendor built a feature your ministry never even authorized.

3
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:14,800
Yeah. Or worse, the vendor claims a critical function was just never asked for.

4
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:19,040
Right. And your team, well, they can't point to the exact document that proves it was.

5
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:20,340
It's a huge problem.

6
00:00:20,940 --> 00:00:29,280
So today's deep dive explores how large scale projects can avoid becoming fragmented, siloed, or, you know, incredibly costly to change down the line

7
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:32,299
by fixing how we track initial requests.

8
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:37,740
And that exact scenario happens because of how human memory and organizational momentum work.

9
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:41,420
Months into a project, memories just degrade.

10
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:49,060
Oh, absolutely. And suppliers might deliberately allow scope creep to bill more hours, or developers simply misinterpret vague legal phrasing.

11
00:00:49,420 --> 00:00:54,620
Right. Which means without a definitive anchor, you just end up with duplicated effort and rekeyed data.

12
00:00:55,420 --> 00:01:03,840
Exactly. And the mechanism to prevent this is called a register of what was asked, a document created before a single line of code is written.

13
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:13,620
Before any design begins at all. Yes. So instead of a loose collection of emails and, uh, meeting notes, this register captures every separate request using the

14
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:16,440
customer's exact words in quotation marks.

15
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:18,540
And it cites the precise source.

16
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:21,680
Right. It makes me think of, like, a software compiler.

17
00:01:21,980 --> 00:01:25,400
If a programmer writes a code script with a single unassigned variable...

18
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,400
The whole program refuses to run.

19
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:34,900
Exactly, until a human debugs it. This register acts like a legal and psychological compiler for project management.

20
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:37,960
That is a highly accurate way to look at it.

21
00:01:38,580 --> 00:01:44,240
It forces an upfront debug of the requirements, binding the supplier to your precise needs.

22
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:49,500
Okay, so to see how this works mechanically, we can look at the fictional country of Progressa.

23
00:01:50,040 --> 00:01:54,940
Right. Progressa and its Higher Education Quality Authority, PHEQA.

24
00:01:55,540 --> 00:02:01,820
The regulations contain some pretty complex, dense text that needs to be translated into actionable system requirements.

25
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:07,140
But having a hyper-specific list doesn't automatically solve the execution problem, does it?

26
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:09,160
Well, no. It takes discipline.

27
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:16,700
Because if I'm reading their regulations, I'm seeing mandates like, uh, number one, apply through PHEQA self-service.

28
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,080
Right. Number two, inspect before recommending.

29
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:21,800
Number three, the minister decides the license.

30
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:25,260
Number four, the register of institutions is open to the public.

31
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:28,780
And number five, a person aggrieved may ask for a review.

32
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:32,140
So all five of those entries are legally binding.

33
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:38,740
Yeah, but if the developers don't understand the legal phrasing of, say, rule number four, they're still gonna build it wrong.

34
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:39,660
Precisely.

35
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:45,080
A developer might build a public register, but leave out the names of the institution owners.

36
00:02:45,480 --> 00:02:48,020
Just because the legal text didn't explicitly mention it.

37
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:53,440
Exactly. That is why creating the register requires two non-negotiable habits.

38
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:56,500
Habit one is one thing per entry.

39
00:02:57,300 --> 00:03:00,640
So if a single sentence of regulation contains three separate mandates-

40
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:02,700
You split it into three distinct entries.

41
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:03,480
You have to.

42
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:09,800
And habit two is to write out in plain words exactly what evidence will show the requirement is met.

43
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:13,640
Okay, so for that second rule, the inspect before recommending one,

44
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:23,660
the plain words translating that for the system build would be something like, um, "No recommendation can be sent to the minister for an application with no recorded inspection."

45
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:27,520
Perfect. It takes the ambiguity completely out of the equation.

46
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:30,520
It establishes a specific behavioral outcome.

47
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:32,540
Exactly. But this process,

48
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:39,000
it inevitably exposes flaws in the original text, like you mentioned rule number five earlier.

49
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:42,100
Right. A person aggrieved may ask for a review.

50
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:48,340
That's a perfect example of a gap. Yes. The rule says they can ask for a review, but it gives absolutely no time limit.

51
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:52,280
And when faced with that gap, the ironclad rule is to never guess.

52
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:57,320
Never guess, because a guessed deadline might make the register look complete.

53
00:03:57,820 --> 00:04:01,880
But building a system around a wrong guess will make it costly to change later.

54
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:05,340
Instead, every gap is flagged as a question.

55
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:08,200
And assigned to a specific owner with a hard deadline.

56
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:09,060
Exactly.

57
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:18,240
If the regulation is silent on how many days a review takes, that question gets assigned to a definitive authority, like a specific director at the ministry.

58
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:20,760
They have to decide before design starts.

59
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:27,800
Yes, exactly. Honestly, though, looking at these massive text dumps and the tedious nature of splitting sentences and finding gaps,

60
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:31,720
this seems like the perfect job to just hand off to an AI.

61
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:35,820
I mean, an AI assistant is incredibly useful for the heavy lifting.

62
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:38,620
Let a large language model parse the regulations.

63
00:04:38,980 --> 00:04:40,600
Just generate the entire register?

64
00:04:41,060 --> 00:04:47,880
Well, it can do that first pass, taking hundreds of pages of legal text and splitting them into isolated entries in minutes.

65
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:51,200
Right. But the AI is strictly proposing.

66
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:54,960
A human analyst has to physically rule on every single entry.

67
00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:58,920
Accepting it, amending it, or setting it aside with a documented reason.

68
00:04:59,340 --> 00:05:04,660
Crucially, yes. And if an entry has no verifiable source attached, it gets struck out immediately.

69
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,260
Wow. That's a really rigorous standard. But I can see how it forces accountability.

70
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:16,900
It does. By demanding one thing per entry using exact words, citing exact sources, and assigning clear owners to resolve gaps,

71
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:21,400
you prevent being locked into one supplier who holds all the institutional knowledge.

72
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:26,880
That upfront discipline is literally the only way to maintain control over a massive build.

73
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:29,480
It removes the interpretation from the supplier.

74
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,960
And keeps the authority exactly where it belongs, with the customer.

75
00:05:33,380 --> 00:05:36,340
Absolutely. Which leaves a fascinating question to consider.

